commit 2291235e3d2c04d7ca5effb4b9c0f8b6cfaa5537
Author: D. Zack Garza <dzackgarza@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Nov 15 14:53:31 2026 +0000

    fix: the points of a finite ordered set hash, and a finite product is found under its family's key (#531)
    
    The interner of finite products and coproducts (df5f484) kept one store
    keyed by the index set's cardinality and compared a new family with every
    stored family of that cardinality, so every binary product compared with
    every earlier binary product.  It could not key by the factors because a
    factor could be a finite ordered set of points that refuse a hash, and a
    key consistent with equality that every such set supplies does not exist
    beyond its cardinality: a mutable vector equals its immutable copy, and no
    total, non-constant invariant agrees with equality across both.
    
    The rule, stated once on FiniteOrderedSets: the points of a finite ordered
    set are values; each hashes, and its hash agrees with its equality.  A set
    is fixed by its points, and what is keyed by a set or its points (the
    inverse of its enumeration, the interned product and coproduct, an interned
    label set) is correct only while the points cannot change.  A point that
    refuses a hash is mutable (a list, a mutable Sage vector: pass the tuple,
    or the vector after set_immutable()) or its owner computes no hash for it
    (a constant hash agrees with every equality).  SET-05 decides the
    capability: one product per equal family needs a key that agrees with
    equality, and a set of mutable points can stop being equal to the set it
    was interned as.
    
    Consequences:
    
    - The interner (set_categories._finite_family_key) keys a family by its
      index set and the multiset of its factors' hashes, and compares by ==
      only the families of its key.  Equal families have one key in either
      construction order; an undecided == builds and keeps both.
    - The equality fallbacks of _on_points, from_indexed (the try at
      finite_ordered_sets.py:415) and _filtered_ordered_set are deleted: each
      builds the hash table of its points, which refuses an unhashable point
      with Python's TypeError.  The query paths that admit an arbitrary
      candidate (index_of, contains on a candidate that refuses a hash, such as
      a mutable vector equal to a point) keep their comparison.
    - interned_labels and its isinstance(label, Hashable) branch are deleted;
      framed_free_modules calls interned_finite_ordered_set.  The unused
      Hashable imports go.
    - IndexedFamily.__hash__'s docstring names the interner's key.  Docstring
      specimens on list labels use tuple labels.
    
    Owners whose points refused a hash, found by running every test directory
    at main with the fallbacks logged, each given a hash that agrees with its
    equality:
    
    - CharacterSets elements: the values on the conjugacy-class
      representatives (their equality is identity on main, #538).
    - Functor (objects of an arrow category and the flag diagrams among the
      isotropic orbit representatives, _ArrowAsFunctor and
      FiniteSequenceDiagram): the identity hash, which agrees with a functor's
      identity equality (Sage's Element comparison; no functor defines __eq__).
    - FormedModuleMorphism (lattice embeddings) declared __hash__ = None; it
      hashes its module map by the module morphisms' own hash, which equal
      pairs (f, h) share.
    - RationalPolyhedralCones parents: the ambient lattice and the engine
      polyhedron, which is what equality compares.
    ConvexPolytopes parents still refuse a hash; that is #491, red at main, and
    the triangle test now stops at facets() with the same TypeError.
    
    The cone hash makes the cone-keyed caches of toric_schemes share a chart
    between equal cones of two toric varieties.  Four tests of
    tests/divisors/test_toric_class_groups.sage, green at main, fail when the
    file runs in one process (each passes alone): #540.  Without the cone hash,
    three edgewalk and cone tests that are green at main fail instead, because a
    finite ordered set of cones is refused.
    
    Suite evidence: every test directory was run at main (with the deleted
    fallbacks logged) and with this repair minus the Functor, cone and formed
    morphism hashes; every test red only with the repair was rerun with the
    complete repair: 36 of 36 pass (tests/lattices, tests/objects,
    tests/engineering).  tests/sets with the touched algebras and toric files:
    34 failed / 143 passed; every failure is red at main except the four toric
    tests of #540.  Not rerun with the complete repair: the whole suite as one pass;
    tests/lattices files after the 240th ran past the 590 s cap at main and
    with the repair.
    
    Tests: the red commit d069ddf.  tests/algebras/test_one_construction_per_label_set
    loses the half that asserted list labels keep their identity.
    
